 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Pre-computation is $O(N^3)$ for the 3D table (where $N$ is the number of discrete scalar values), but runtime is $O(1)$ per segment via table lookup [S3].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N^3)$ for the 3D LUT. If $l$ is constant, it reduces to $O(N^2)$ [S2, unofficial][S3].
+- **Time Complexity:** Pre-computation requires evaluating integrals for all combinations of $(s_f, s_b, l)$, which scales with the discretization of the scalar range and segment length [S3]. Runtime is $O(1)$ per segment via table lookup [S3].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Requires a 3D table indexed by $(s_f, s_b, l)$ [S3]. If $l$ is constant, the table simplifies to 2D $(s_f, s_b)$ [S2, unofficial].
 - **Measured Performance:** Enables real-time raycasting on GPUs by moving the expensive integration to pre-processing [S2, unofficial][S4, book].
 - **Strengths:** Captures high frequencies of the transfer function better than zero-order integration; reduces artifacts from piecewise-constant assumptions [S1, unofficial][S3].
